Much of this activity will take place outside the visibility of the target organization, making detection of this behavior difficult. Detection efforts may be focused on related stages of the adversary lifecycle, such as during Defense Evasion or Command and Control."}, "dictionary_item_removed": {"root['x_mitre_deprecated']": false, "root['x_mitre_is_subtechnique']": false}, "values_changed": {"root['modified']": {"new_value": "2022-04-25 14:00:00.188000+00:00", "old_value": "2025-10-24 17:49:34.675000+00:00"}, "root['x_mitre_attack_spec_version']": {"new_value": "2.1.0", "old_value": "3.2.0"}}}

{"dictionary_item_added": {"root['x_mitre_data_sources']": ["Internet Scan: Response Content", "Malware Repository: Malware Content", "Certificate: Certificate Registration", "Malware Repository: Malware Metadata"], "root['x_mitre_detection']": "Consider analyzing malware for features that may be associated with malware providers, such as compiler used, debugging artifacts, code similarities, or even group identifiers associated with specific Malware-as-a-Service (MaaS) offerings. Malware repositories can also be used to identify additional samples associated with the developers and the adversary utilizing their services. Identifying overlaps in malware use by different adversaries may indicate malware was obtained by the adversary rather than developed by them. In some cases, identifying overlapping characteristics in malware used by different adversaries may point to a shared quartermaster.(Citation: FireEyeSupplyChain) Malware repositories can also be used to identify features of tool use associated with an adversary, such as watermarks in [Cobalt Strike](https://attack.mitre.org/software/S0154) payloads.(Citation: Analyzing CS Dec 2020)

Consider use of services that may aid in the tracking of newly issued certificates and/or certificates in use on sites across the Internet. In some cases it may be possible to pivot on known pieces of certificate information to uncover other adversary infrastructure.(Citation: Splunk Kovar Certificates 2017) Some server-side components of adversary tools may have default values set for SSL/TLS certificates.(Citation: Recorded Future Beacon Certificates)

Much of this activity will take place outside the visibility of the target organization, making detection of this behavior difficult. Detection efforts may be focused on related stages of the adversary lifecycle, such as during Defense Evasion or Command and Control."}, "dictionary_item_removed": {"root['x_mitre_deprecated']": false, "root['x_mitre_is_subtechnique']": false}, "values_changed": {"root['modified']": {"new_value": "2022-04-25 14:00:00.188000+00:00", "old_value": "2025-10-24 17:49:24.545000+00:00"}, "root['external_references'][4]['description']": {"new_value": "Insikt Group. (2019, June 18). A Multi-Method Approach to Identifying Rogue Cobalt Strike Servers. Retrieved October 16, 2020.", "old_value": "Insikt Group. (2019, June 18). A Multi-Method Approach to Identifying Rogue Cobalt Strike Servers. Retrieved September 16, 2024.", "new_path": "root['external_references'][7]['description']"}, "root['external_references'][4]['url']": {"new_value": "https://www.recordedfuture.com/cobalt-strike-servers/", "old_value": "https://www.recordedfuture.com/research/cobalt-strike-servers", "new_path": "root['external_references'][7]['url']"}, "root['x_mitre_attack_spec_version']": {"new_value": "2.1.0", "old_value": "3.2.0"}}}

